Bobby Brown wrote:

> The fun part is surviving long enough to become indestructible. Is this
> what drives people to pursue conducts? Couldn't the end-times be
> improved? Wading through hundreds of opponents is not challenging, it is
> dull. When tele/jumping is a valid "strategy," you know there's a serious
> problem.

People pursue conducts when they start to become really good at the
game, and seek a way to maintain the challenge.  Most conducts must be
executed from turn one, so they're not something you can apply to a
game mid-way through.


> Demogorgon, Famine, Death and Pestilence are the only interesting things
> about the end-times. There should be less of the mundane and more of that
> sort of challenge.

Actually, the elemental planes are quite cool, each of them gives the
player a radically different challenge than the main game.  Earth must
be dug through, Air is essentially a big room with a levitation
requirement and really fast monsters (the air elementals there can be
lethal), Fire is also bigroomish and filled with lava, and Water is
coolest of all since its entire geometry changes as you move around.

There *are* dangerous monsters in the game besides the ones you give.
Archons can be troublesome if enough appear.  Liches, eels, mind
flayers and disenchanters are frequent genocide targets for a reason.
And then there's the Wizard himself, who can be quite troublesome if
the player runs out of death charges against him.  Usually that doesn't
happen though.

